# Normalize to LF in the repository AND in the working tree, on every
# platform. Development happens on Windows with core.autocrlf=true, but every
# artifact this repo produces runs on Linux: CI, the python:3.12-slim image,
# Cloud Run. Without `eol=lf` a Windows checkout hands back CRLF, which
# contradicts .editorconfig and reintroduces exactly the class of mismatch
# that corrupted model.txt below.
#
# Verified 2026-08-03 before adding: every tracked file already stores LF in
# the index, so this renormalizes nothing -- it only fixes checkout.
* text=auto eol=lf

# --- exemption, must stay AFTER the rule above (last match wins) ------------
#
# model_bundle/**'s files are checksummed byte-for-byte in bundle_meta.json
# and verified at bundle-load time (src/inference/bundle.py). Without this,
# core.autocrlf=true silently rewrites LF -> CRLF on checkout for any file
# git doesn't detect as binary by content sniffing -- which model.txt
# (LightGBM's native text format) is not, since it doesn't look binary the
# way dest_state.parquet does. That corrupted model.txt's bytes on a Windows
# checkout while leaving its sha256 in bundle_meta.json pointing at the
# original LF content, so load_bundle() correctly refused to serve it.
# Marking everything here `-text` disables EOL conversion unconditionally,
# on every platform, for every file in the bundle.
#
# `!eol` is as load-bearing as `-text`: git's docs state that setting `eol`
# "effectively sets the text attribute", so leaving the global `eol=lf` above
# to apply here could re-enable the very conversion `-text` disables. `!eol`
# returns the attribute to unspecified for these paths. Verified below with
# `git check-attr`, not assumed.
model_bundle/** -text !eol
